Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: wm8962: restore SYSCLK_ENA after bclk setup when mic detect is active

wm8962_mic_detect() force-enables the "SYSCLK" DAPM pin while a jack is
registered, so DAPM sees the SYSCLK supply as permanently powered and never
rewrites its enable bit (WM8962_CLOCKING2 bit 5).

On a cold stream start the bias level goes to SND_SOC_BIAS_PREPARE, where
wm8962_configure_bclk() briefly enables SYSCLK to read DSPCLK_DIV and then
clears SYSCLK_ENA again because the bias level is not yet ON. DAPM never
turns it back on, so the DACs and the speaker and headphone amplifiers are
all clocked off: the PCM runs normally but nothing is audible. A warm
restart (bias already ON) skips the clear, which makes the problem look
intermittent. A runtime resume also forces the bit off the same way.

Re-enable SYSCLK at the end of the PREPARE step whenever mic detection (and
therefore the forced SYSCLK pin) is active.

Observed on Microsoft Surface RT (Tegra 30, WM8962 on i2c bus 4, address
0x1a): R8 reads 0x09c7 (bit 5 clear) 0.4 s into a cold playback and stays
0x09e7 once the bit is restored.

---
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/wm8962.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/wm8962.c
@@ -2585,6 +2585,7 @@
 				 enum snd_soc_bias_level level)
 {
 	struct snd_soc_dapm_context *dapm = snd_soc_component_to_dapm(component);
+	struct wm8962_priv *wm8962 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
 
 	switch (level) {
 	case SND_SOC_BIAS_ON:
@@ -2596,6 +2597,17 @@
 				    WM8962_VMID_SEL_MASK, 0x80);
 
 		wm8962_configure_bclk(component);
+
+		/*
+		 * With mic detection active, the "SYSCLK" DAPM pin is forced on,
+		 * so DAPM never sees a power-up transition for it and will not
+		 * rewrite SYSCLK_ENA. wm8962_configure_bclk() above (and a
+		 * runtime resume) leave the bit cleared in the hardware, which
+		 * silences every output. Put it back.
+		 */
+		if (wm8962->jack)
+			snd_soc_component_update_bits(component, WM8962_CLOCKING2,
+					WM8962_SYSCLK_ENA_MASK, WM8962_SYSCLK_ENA);
 		break;
 
 	case SND_SOC_BIAS_STANDBY:
